**Critical** — Non-functional pagination control shipped to users

**What:** A brand-new pager (‹ / › buttons, "01 of 01" label) was added to the Response Queue with no `onClick` handlers and no pagination state. The count line also mixes `total` (all reviews) with `queue.length` (the filtered set) — with a filter active it reads e.g. "Showing 1–3 of 412 items", which is factually wrong.

**Why it matters:** The buttons carry `aria-label`s so they're announced to assistive tech as actionable controls, but clicking them does nothing. This is a real, user-visible defect in a restyle MR framed as presentational-only.

**Recommendation:**
```tsx
// Either wire real pagination state:
const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
// ...onClick={() => setPage(p => Math.max(1, p - 1))}

// Or remove the block entirely and keep:
Showing 1–{queue.length} of {queue.length} items
```

Confidence: 100/100 | Found by: MR Analyst, Codebase Reviewer (independently confirmed)

**Critical** — Unrecognised citation engine silently mislabeled as ChatGPT

**What:** `engineIconKey()` falls through to the raw (lowercased) engine name for anything it doesn't recognise, and `getLLMIcon`'s `default:` case (`components/GEO/LLMIcons.tsx:455-456`) renders the ChatGPT logo for any unmatched key.

**Why it matters:** Any engine this mapper doesn't know — a newly added engine, a renamed display string — will render with the ChatGPT logo. The Citation Gap / Mentions tables then visually assert a citation from an engine that never actually cited the source. The previous text pill was always correct; this regresses data accuracy for the sake of the visual refresh.

**Recommendation:**
```tsx
const key = engineIconKey(engine);
return key ? getLLMIcon(key, { sx: { fontSize: 18 } }) : (
  <Box sx={perceptionEnginePillSx(isDark)}>{engine}</Box>
);
```
Make the fallback explicit (return `null`/`undefined` from `engineIconKey` for unmapped names) rather than silently defaulting to a specific brand's logo.

Confidence: 88/100 | Found by: Codebase Reviewer

**Important** — Shared chip helper's hardcoded gold styling breaks risk-colour semantics in an untouched dialog

**What:** `localPageChipSx` now forces `color`/`bgcolor`/`border` plus a `'& .MuiChip-label'` override to a gold treatment, at specificity that outranks MUI's `color` prop. This helper is consumed by four components, only one of which (`CrawlPanel.tsx`) is in this MR's scope. In `components/LocalPresence/GBPPreviewModal.tsx:72-77`, a risk-level `<Chip color={riskColor}>` (HIGH=error/red, MEDIUM=warning/amber, LOW=success/green) now renders identically gold regardless of risk level.

**Why it matters:** `GBPPreviewModal` is a destructive-change confirmation dialog — losing the risk-colour signal there is a real usability/safety regression in a file this MR never intended to touch.

**Recommendation:**
```ts
export const localPageChipSx = (isDark: boolean, tone: 'gold' | 'inherit' = 'inherit') => {
  // only apply the gold color/bgcolor/border override when tone === 'gold'
};
```
Pass `'gold'` only from `CrawlPanel`'s services chips; leave the other three call sites (`GBPPreviewModal`, `PostsScheduler`, `ReviewsPanel`) on their existing semantics.

Confidence: 88/100 | Found by: Codebase Reviewer

**Minor** — Keyboard-inert clickable cards

**What:** The new directory cards apply `role="button"` and `tabIndex={0}` when clickable, but there's no `onKeyDown` handler — only the `onClick`.

**Why it matters:** The card takes keyboard focus and is announced by assistive tech as a button, but Enter/Space do nothing (WCAG 2.1.1). This repo's ESLint config has no `jsx-a11y` plugin, so CI won't catch it.

**Recommendation:**
```tsx
onKeyDown={(e) => {
  if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
    e.preventDefault();
    onViewReviews?.(platform.id);
  }
}}
```
Or simpler: render the card as `component="button" type="button"` and get correct keyboard behaviour for free.

Confidence: 85/100 | Found by: Codebase Reviewer

